<description>
Draw each colored marker's fixed trail, then turn red/vertical trail crossings into yellow down-left diagonals.

Input structure
- A black background contains one pixel of each present marker color drawn from blue, red, green, and pink.
- The grey and yellow colors are absent from the input and are used only in the output trail drawing.

Rule
- Blue paints two grey pixels to the right and places a blue endpoint one row above the second grey pixel.
- Red paints three grey pixels to the left and places a red endpoint four columns left.
- Green paints two grey pixels downward and places a green endpoint three rows down.
- Pink paints five grey pixels upward and places a pink endpoint six rows up.
- Where a red horizontal segment crosses a green or pink vertical segment, paint yellow there and continue yellow diagonally down-left to the grid edge.

Key insight
- Marker color identifies a fixed motion/trail, and yellow is the collision effect between the red westward trail and a vertical trail.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "single colored marker pixels on the black background",
  "path": "color-coded fixed routes: blue goes two cells right then one up, red goes four left, green goes three down, and pink goes six up",
  "trajectory": "paint intermediate route pixels grey and place a same-color marker at each endpoint, preserving the original markers",
  "transform": "where a red horizontal segment crosses a green or pink vertical segment, recolor the crossing yellow and extend yellow diagonally down-left to the grid edge"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 9-11 rows x 9-11 cols, always square [observed]
  background: black (0)
  markers:
    count: 1-4 single-pixel markers, any non-empty subset of {blue(1), red(2), green(3), pink(6)}
    each_unique: exactly 1 pixel per color present
  trails_per_color:
    blue: 2 grey right of source, endpoint = source color at (r-1, c+2); needs c+2 < W and r >= 1
    red: 3 grey left of source, endpoint = source color at (r, c-4); needs c >= 4
    green: 2 grey below source, endpoint = source color at (r+3, c); needs r+3 < H
    pink: 5 grey above source, endpoint = source color at (r-6, c); needs r >= 6
  segments_for_crossing:
    red: horizontal span cols [c-4 .. c] at row r
    green: vertical span rows [r .. r+3] at col c
    pink: vertical span rows [r-6 .. r] at col c
  crossings: where red horizontal intersects a green/pink vertical (cell must be bg in input)
  yellow_diagonal: from each crossing (r,c), paint yellow going (r+1,c-1), (r+1,c-1)... to grid edge
  non_overlap: all trail cells, endpoints, and yellow diagonal cells must be bg(0) in input; no endpoint overlaps another endpoint or trail (crossing grey cells may overlap between red and vertical trail)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size N x N, N in [9..11].
    2. Choose a non-empty subset of {blue, red, green, pink}.
    3. For each chosen color, pick a source position satisfying trail-space bounds (blue needs c <= W-3 and r >= 1; red needs c >= 4; green needs r <= H-4; pink needs r >= 6).
    4. Compute all trail cells, endpoints, and segments. Verify no cell collisions (trails/endpoints all land on distinct bg cells, except red-vertical crossing overlap on grey).
    5. If red and a vertical marker both present, check for crossings; compute yellow diagonal from each crossing down-left to grid edge. Verify diagonal cells don't hit any source/endpoint.
    6. If any collision, retry placement. Acceptance rate is high on 9-11 grids with 1-4 markers.
    7. Input = black grid with only the source pixels placed. Output = input + grey trails + colored endpoints + yellow diagonals.
```
